        'home.card2.text': "Do worries dominate your thoughts, or do you increasingly avoid situations that make you anxious? Together, we explore what triggers and maintains your anxiety. Building on this, we develop concrete steps that help you gradually approach anxiety-provoking situations again and gain more freedom to act.",
        'home.card3.title': "Distressing and traumatic experiences",
        'home.card3.text': "Some experiences have a long-lasting impact – for example through intrusive memories, intense tension or the feeling of no longer being safe. Psychotherapeutic treatment can help you understand these effects and process what you have been through. Which approach and treatment setting are appropriate is something we clarify carefully at the start.",
        'home.card4.title': "Exhaustion, overwhelm and life changes",
        'home.card4.text': "Perhaps you keep functioning on the outside but feel increasingly drained on the inside. Or a separation, a career change or another burden has thrown your previous balance off. Together, we look at what is weighing on you, which needs are getting too little space and which changes are possible.",
        'home.services.note': "<strong>Good to know:</strong> Not every difficult phase of life requires psychotherapy. In the initial consultation, we therefore clarify whether there is a mental health condition that requires treatment and which kind of support fits your concerns.",

        'home.why.eyebrow': "Why psychotherapy?",
        'home.why.title': "You don't have to reach your limits before seeking support",
        'home.why.text': "It is perfectly fine to come to the first conversation feeling uncertain. Together, we will find out which kind of support makes sense for you.",
        'home.why.p1': "Perhaps you have been wondering for a while whether psychotherapy might be right for you. At the same time, you may feel that you should cope with your difficulties on your own, or that other people have bigger problems.",
        'home.why.p2': "What matters, however, is how <strong>you</strong> are doing. If anxiety, low mood or other symptoms persist and affect your everyday life, your relationships or your quality of life, it can be helpful to seek professional support. You don't need to know a diagnosis for that.",
        'home.why.p3': "Mental health conditions are not a personal failure. Their development cannot be reduced to a single cause: physical factors, personal experiences and social circumstances can all interact. Therapy is therefore not about assigning blame, but about understanding your situation as well as possible.",
        'home.why.p4': "In our sessions, we take time for what is on your mind. We look at recurring difficulties, the ways you have tried to cope so far and the strengths you can already draw on. From this, we develop concrete steps that fit your needs and possibilities.",
        'home.why.p5': "The goal is not simply for you to function even better. It is about reducing distress and regaining more opportunities to shape your life according to your own ideas.",

        'home.cbt.eyebrow': "What is cognitive behavioural therapy?",
        'home.cbt.title': "Understanding how difficulties arise – and finding out what can change",
        'home.cbt.p1': "Cognitive behavioural therapy looks at the interplay between thoughts, feelings, physical reactions and behaviour. Your previous experiences and your current life circumstances are also taken into account. Together, we explore how your symptoms developed and which factors contribute to keeping them going.",
        'home.cbt.p2': "<strong>“Cognitive”</strong> refers to our thinking: for example, how we assess situations, what expectations we have and what meaning we give to our experiences. The <strong>behavioural part</strong> focuses on how we act – and which new experiences become possible when we act differently.",
        'home.cbt.p3': "This is not about talking yourself out of difficulties or simply switching off unpleasant feelings. In my work, I want to examine with you which perspectives and reactions help you and which have come to restrict you. We look for alternatives that don't just sound convincing in conversation, but can also be put into practice in your everyday life.",
        'home.cbt.p4': "Your life story remains important. It can help explain why certain patterns developed. At the same time, we focus on the present: What do you need today, and where can change begin?",
        'home.cbt.why.title': "Why I work with cognitive behavioural therapy",
        'home.cbt.why.p1': "Cognitive behavioural therapy is one of the most thoroughly researched psychotherapeutic approaches. It is used, among other things, for depression, anxiety disorders and obsessive-compulsive disorder. Which approach makes sense, however, always depends on the specific symptoms and the individual situation.",
        'home.cbt.why.p2': "What I particularly value about this approach is that it allows us to combine understanding with practical change. We don't just talk about what is difficult – we also develop ways of dealing with it differently.",
        'home.cbt.why.p3': "It is important to me that you can understand why I suggest a particular exercise or approach. We agree on goals, review our progress and adjust the treatment if something isn't helpful. Your feedback is explicitly part of this.",
        'home.cbt.practice.title': "What does this look like in practice?",
        'home.cbt.practice.p1': "Depending on your concerns, we might, for example, work on re-examining distressing appraisals, gradually returning to situations you have been avoiding, or bringing helpful activities back into your everyday life. The choice depends on your symptoms and the goals we have agreed on together.",
        'home.cbt.practice.p2': "Between sessions, you can try out selected steps. The point is not to complete tasks perfectly. Difficulties or doubts also provide valuable insights that we can pick up on together in the next session.",
        'home.cbt.practice.p3': "Therapy is not meant to dictate how you should live. It is meant to help you recognise your options more clearly and use them more consciously.",

